speechtext:
Madam Speaker, a devastating hail storm recently struck the Holland Marsh area of Ontario, causing millions of dollars of damage to vegetable crops as well as destroying hundreds of acres of land and completely wiping out many farmers. This 297region is known across Canada for its rich market garden industry and its vegetable storage and processing facilities. The reclamation of this rich farmland is one of the remarkable achievements of this century. I therefore move, seconded by the hon. member
for Simcoe North (Mr. Lewis) and the hon. member for St. Catharines (Mr. Reid):
That the Minister of Agriculture or his representative personally visit the Holland Marsh to see first hand the damage caused to this vegetable capital of Ontario with a view to working with his provincial counterpart to determine whether or not the region should be declared a disaster area.

There is only one answer, Madam Speaker. The minister laughs. The fact that he is asking Canadians to pay six per cent more in taxes, is asking people on spouses' allowance and those who are disabled to pay more through the loss of indexation, and is asking Canadians who cannot afford it to pay for his mistakes-that is why the minister must resign.
Madam Speaker could I move;
That the debate be now adjourned.
